  "response": "**Setup (Bayes' theorem):**\n\n- Prevalence: P(D) = 1/500 = 0.002, so P(¬D) = 0.998\n- Sensitivity: P(+|D) = 0.98\n- False positive rate: P(+|¬D) = 1 − specificity = 0.05\n\n**Calculation:**\n\nP(+) = P(+|D)P(D) + P(+|¬D)P(¬D)\n= (0.98 × 0.002) + (0.05 × 0.998)\n= 0.00196 + 0.0499\n= 0.05186\n\nP(D|+) = P(+|D)P(D) / P(+) = 0.00196 / 0.05186 = 0.0377941…\n\nConverting to a percentage: 3.77941…% → rounds to **3.78%**.\n\n**Sanity check:** This makes intuitive sense — with a rare condition (1/500) and a 5% false-positive rate, false positives (≈49.9 per 1000 tested) vastly outnumber true positives (≈1.96 per 1000 tested), so most positive results are false alarms, consistent with a low ~3.8% posterior probability.\n\nFINAL ANSWER: 3.78",
